  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Native API Abuse — Direct Syscall ELF", "technique": "T1106", "what_happened": "At 2027-03-18 09:02:11 CET, auditd on LNX-WEB-184 (10.108.57.184) recorded execve of a static ELF binary dropped to /tmp/syscall_stub_20270318_090155 — a custom-compiled C program that calls syscall(SYS_openat) and syscall(SYS_write) directly, completely bypassing the glibc audit-hook layer. The binary was compiled on-host from /tmp/syscall_src_20270318_090155.c seconds before execution. auditd records the raw syscall= numbers regardless of the libc bypass (that is the point of auditd at kernel level): syscall=59 (execve) entry + syscall=257 (openat) + syscall=1 (write) all emitted under the same PID. Wazuh rule 92754 fired: 'Direct syscall execution (static ELF + auditd)' at level 12, MITRE T1106.", "chain_reason": "auditd syscall= entries for execve of a file matching /tmp/syscall_stub_* with ELF magic — compiler artefact pattern plus direct openat/write syscalls without corresponding glibc wrapper calls triggers rule 92754. Static ELF in /tmp with direct syscall invocation is the hallmark of libc-bypass evasion used by Cobalt Strike beacons and Sliver C2 implants.", "first_seen": "2027-03-18T09:02:11+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Native API Abuse — LD_PRELOAD Injection Attempt", "technique": "T1106", "what_happened": "At 09:05:44 CET (~3.5 minutes after the direct-syscall stub), the attacker compiled a minimal shared-object stub (/tmp/ld_preload_src_20270318_090155.c → /tmp/ld_preload_20270318_090155.so) and invoked: LD_PRELOAD=/tmp/ld_preload_20270318_090155.so /bin/ls. The LD_PRELOAD mechanism forces the dynamic linker to load the attacker-controlled .so before any standard library, allowing interception of any libc function call (open, read, write, getenv, etc.). Although this particular invocation targets /bin/ls (a harmless pivot target), auditd records the execve with envp[]=LD_PRELOAD= — the environment-variable injection pattern is the forensic signal. Wazuh rule 92755 fired: 'LD_PRELOAD injection attempt' at level 14, MITRE T1106, CRITICAL.", "chain_reason": "auditd execve event with envp[] containing LD_PRELOAD= pointing to a .so artifact in /tmp/ is an unambiguous library-injection indicator. LD_PRELOAD from /tmp is a textbook persistence and hooking mechanism — dropping a custom .so to a world-writable directory and injecting it into a process intercepting calls like getpwuid, pam_authenticate, or open enables credential theft and syscall hooking.", "first_seen": "2027-03-18T09:05:44+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Defense Evasion — FIM Detection of Malicious Artefacts in /tmp", "technique": "T1106", "what_happened": "At 09:08:22 CET, Wazuh FIM completed a real-time scan cycle and flagged four new files created in /tmp/ since the last baseline: /tmp/syscall_src_20270318_090155.c (C source), /tmp/syscall_stub_20270318_090155 (ELF binary, mode 0755 executable), /tmp/ld_preload_src_20270318_090155.c (shared-object C source), and /tmp/ld_preload_20270318_090155.so (.so shared library, mode 0755). Rule 52103 fired: 'FIM: ELF and .so artifacts in /tmp' at level 10, MITRE T1106. The four files collectively represent the full attack toolkit: source code used to compile on-target (LOLBin: gcc available on the host), the compiled ELF, the LD_PRELOAD stub source, and the compiled injection library. Their timestamps are tightly clustered (09:02:00–09:05:50) confirming compilation and execution happened in a single ~4-minute operator session.", "chain_reason": "FIM syscheck.path matching /tmp/*.so and /tmp/syscall_stub_* with syscheck.event=added and executable permissions detected during a real-time FIM scan cycle. The four-file cluster (two C sources + one ELF + one .so, all timestamped within 4 minutes) is the forensic signature of on-host compilation for evasion purposes.", "first_seen": "2027-03-18T09:08:22+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence / Continued Presence", "technique": "T1106", "what_happened": "At 09:14:11 CET, auditd recorded a second execve of /tmp/syscall_stub_20270318_090155 — the attacker re-ran the direct-syscall binary. The 8.5-minute gap between first and second execution suggests the operator paused to review output (the stub reads /etc/passwd via direct SYS_openat) and decided to re-execute, possibly with a different target file or to confirm persistence viability. The FIM scan cycle at 09:16:44 did NOT flag new files — the artefacts remain in /tmp/ and have not been cleaned, indicating the attacker is not yet in cleanup phase. Wazuh rule 92754 fired a second time.", "chain_reason": "Second auditd execve event for the same /tmp/syscall_stub_* binary within the FIM 10-minute scan window, with no corresponding file-deletion event in syscheck — artefacts persist. The re-execution pattern combined with /etc/passwd as the syscall target (sensitive file read) elevates confidence this is an active hands-on-keyboard session, not a one-shot automated dropper.", "first_seen": "2027-03-18T09:14:11+0100"}
